course: dictionary
lesson: 7959
title: Recently means not long ago
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Recently means a short time ago, or in the period not long before now. It does not tell you the exact time. «I've been tired recently» means over the last few days or weeks, not years ago.
explain: Look at the words around it for clues. «I have recently started swimming lessons» tells you the swimming began not long before now, because the present perfect («have started») links the past to today.
explain: Recently often comes in phrases: until recently, only recently, quite recently, more recently. Each one points to a time not far from now. «More recently» means closer to now than something else.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. Could you say «not long ago» instead? If yes, recently fits. Remember it is vague: it does not mean exactly one minute or exactly one day.
fact: Recently means a short time ago, or in the period not long before now.
fact: Recently is an adverb. It tells us when something happened.
fact: «Only recently» and «quite recently» both mean not long ago.
fact: «Until recently» means up to a short time ago, but not any more.
fact: «More recently» means at a later time than something before it, and closer to now.
exercise: «I have recently started swimming lessons.» What does recently tell us? | It happened a long time ago | It never happens | *It happened not long ago | It will happen tomorrow
exercise: Ella reads «until recently» in a book. What does it mean? | *Up to a short time ago, but not any more | It will start soon | It has always been like this | It is happening right now
exercise: «Dogs can smell which dogs passed by a lamp-post, and how recently.» What does how recently ask? | Where the dog lives | *How long ago the dog passed | What colour the dog was | How big the dog was
test: Which word means a short time ago? | *Recently | Always | Forever | Tomorrow
test: «The shop has only recently opened.» What does only recently mean? | Never | In the distant past | Every day | *Not long ago
exam: Ella says recently is a fancy word for yesterday. Is she right? | No, recently means in the future | Yes, recently means exactly one day ago | *No, recently means a short time ago, but it does not say exactly when | Yes, recently and yesterday mean exactly the same
